  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "Triangle \\(BAD\\) is right-angled at \\(B.\\) On \\(AD\\) there is a point \\(C\\) for which \\(AC=CD\\) and \\(AB=BC.\\) The magnitude of angle \\(DAB,\\) in degrees, is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(67\\dfrac12\\)",
    "B": "\\(60\\)",
    "C": "\\(45\\)",
    "D": "\\(30\\)",
    "E": "\\(22\\dfrac12\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The condition \\(AC=CD\\) says that \\(C\\) is the midpoint of \\(AD\\). Because \\(\\triangle BAD\\) is right-angled at \\(B\\), the side \\(AD\\) is its hypotenuse.\n\nThe midpoint of a right triangle's hypotenuse is equally distant from all three vertices. Therefore\n\\[\n AC=BC=CD.\n\\]\nThe given equality \\(AB=BC\\) now shows that \\(AB=BC=CA\\), so \\(\\triangle ABC\\) is equilateral and \\(\\angle CAB=60^\\circ\\).\n\nSince \\(C\\) lies on segment \\(AD\\), the rays \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) coincide. Hence \\(\\angle DAB=\\angle CAB=60^\\circ\\), choice B.",
